e in at home  that's the people who don't have the  solar panels can come here connect your  devices and so that they're not Stranded  the Puerto Rican people will tell you it  is the communities coming together that  is helping them survive and that takes  us all the way back to the Good Deeds of  Carlos Rodriguez  one month and a half no water no water  no water and she's just expecting you  and sometimes  no light meet Victoria Ortiz she's a  stroke survivor who lives alone in Vega  Alta struggling to make ends meet  she told us softly how grateful she was  to get a helping hand from a happy Giver  I believe that the future of Puerto Rico  lies in its Community organizing because  when it's the people themselves that are  doing it you see a lot of successes and  even if the general picture might look a  little dim  these communities are showing the path  of what's possible  um David beg now you have done it again  you know you always find these things  that no matter how dire a situation is  there is always some kind of light  whether it's somebody famous like Bad  Bunny who certainly goes above and  beyond always for Puerto Rico to people  who we didn't know until today  that's the takeaway my friend as bad as  it may seem and the suffering is so real  there is a resilience and a smile Gail I  went into a flood zone this week and  when we left a man in his 80s handed me  a big old bag of avocados with a smile  on his face he had no power he had no  water in his home was flooded but he  wanted us to leave with something that  represents the Puerto Rican Spirit yes  David I mean you know that Spirit well  uh your coverage there began five years  ago you've been back consistently in  that time I'm curious on the end of uh  this trip uh how you're feeling about  the the Island's progress toward where  it wants to be your overall thoughts  where it wants to be overall thoughts it  wants to be less reliant on a government  that has consistently failed them and  promis